        '["hero","article-body","call-to-action"]'::jsonb, 'pending');

INSERT INTO page_components (id, page_id, slot_name, position, component_id, content_data, build_status)
SELECT gen_random_uuid(), p.id, 'hero', 1, cc.id, $HEROJSON${"headline": "AI in Hiring: The Widest Trust Gap We Found in Any Sector", "subheadline": "70% of hiring managers trust AI to make faster, better hiring decisions. Only 8% of job seekers call it fair. What actually closes that gap.", "background_image": "/assets/images/hero.jpg"}$HEROJSON$::jsonb, 'deployed'
FROM pages p, content_components cc
WHERE p.site_id='4851f6fc-71cf-4160-a270-e03d6d3e0732' AND p.name='ai-data-trust-in-hiring-and-hr' AND cc.name='hero';

INSERT INTO page_components (id, page_id, slot_name, position, component_id, content_data, build_status)
SELECT gen_random_uuid(), p.id, 'article-body', 2, cc.id, jsonb_build_object('content', $ARTBODY$<p>The last of three deep dives following <a href="/blog/can-you-trust-ai-with-your-data.html">our overview on trusting AI with data across industries</a>. Every sector in that research showed some gap between the people deploying AI and the people affected by it. Hiring produced the widest gap we found, by a wide margin.</p><h2>The Single Starkest Number in This Research</h2><p>Greenhouse's 2025 AI in Hiring study surveyed 4,136 people across the US, UK, Ireland and Germany — roughly 2,900 job seekers and 1,236 recruiters and hiring managers — and produced a finding stark enough to be the headline of its own press release:</p><blockquote>"70% of Hiring Managers Trust AI to Make Faster and Better Hiring Decisions, Only 8% of Job Seekers Call it Fair" — Greenhouse, <em>An AI Trust Crisis</em>, November 2025</blockquote><p>Every other trust gap in this series — healthcare's 50-point gap between AI users and non-users, financial services' gap between adoption and governance — is a gap between two groups looking at the same evidence from different positions. This one is different in kind: 87% of organisations now use AI somewhere in their hiring process, so the hiring manager's trust is trust in a tool they selected, configured, and can see the reasoning behind. The candidate's distrust is distrust in a tool applied to them, by someone else, whose reasoning they typically cannot see at all. That is not a difference of opinion about the same facts. It is two groups with structurally different access to the facts.</p><div class="trust-chart" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart: trust in AI hiring decisions. Hiring managers who trust AI to make faster and better decisions, 70%. Job seekers who call it fair, 8%."><svg viewBox="0 0 720 220" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="width:100%;height:auto;font-family:'Inter',-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,sans-serif;">
<text x="0" y="20" style="font-size:14px;font-weight:700;fill:#1A1A1A;">The hiring trust gap</text>
<text x="0" y="38" style="font-size:11px;fill:#5A5751;">Same tool, the person who chose it and the person evaluated by it</text>
<g style="font-size:12px;fill:#1A1A1A;">
<text x="0" y="82">Hiring managers who trust it</text>
<rect x="260" y="66" width="336" height="26" style="fill:#0D0D0D;"/><text x="606" y="84" style="font-weight:700;">70%</text>
<text x="0" y="140">Job seekers who call it fair</text>
<rect x="260" y="124" width="38" height="26" style="fill:#836E32;"/><text x="308" y="142" style="font-weight:700;">8%</text>
</g>
<line x1="260" y1="58" x2="260" y2="164" style="stroke:#E4DFD5;stroke-width:1;"/>
<text x="0" y="190" style="font-size:10px;fill:#5A5751;">Source: Greenhouse, An AI Trust Crisis: AI in Hiring, November 2025 (n=4,136)</text>
</svg></div><h2>The Irony Sitting Inside the Data</h2><p>The same study found 82% of hiring managers say they are concerned about candidates using AI in their own job search — writing cover letters, prepping interview answers, polishing a resume. Sixty percent report seeing AI-generated resumes or cover letters directly, and half have observed candidates apparently using AI during the interview itself. The tool is a legitimate efficiency gain when the employer runs it, and a credibility problem when the candidate does. Eighty-six percent of hiring managers say AI now makes it harder to verify whether submitted materials genuinely reflect a candidate's own abilities — which is a striking thing for an industry to say about a technology it has adopted at an 87% rate itself.</p><p>Disclosure practices make the asymmetry concrete. Only 35% of companies say they always disclose their AI use to the candidates being evaluated by it; 20% say they never disclose it at all. A candidate being screened, ranked, or rejected by an algorithm has, in one case out of five, no way of even knowing that happened.</p><h2>What Candidates Actually Think, in Their Own Numbers</h2><p>Gartner's survey of 2,918 job candidates, published July 2025, puts a precise number on the candidate side: just 26% trust that AI will evaluate them fairly — even though 52% believe AI is already screening their application in some form. That gap, between how often candidates think they're being screened by AI and how few trust the result, is itself a trust problem independent of whether the screening is any good. On the employer side of the same question, a ResumeBuilder survey of 948 business leaders (October 2024) found 67% acknowledge their own AI hiring tools could introduce bias — 47% naming age bias specifically, 44% socioeconomic bias, 30% gender bias, and 26% racial bias. The concern is not confined to candidates; two-thirds of the companies deploying these tools share it, and can name the specific kind.</p><h2>The One Finding That Points Toward a Fix</h2><p>Not every model of AI-assisted hiring produces the same distrust. Dice's <em>Trust Gap in Tech Hiring 2025</em> report — a survey of 319 US tech professionals conducted June–July 2025 — found 68% distrust a fully AI-driven hiring process, against 80% who trust a human-driven one. But a hybrid model, where AI assists a human decision-maker rather than replacing one, earns trust from 46% of candidates: more than triple the trust level of an AI-only process. That is close to the same pattern found in healthcare in the second article of this series: trust rises sharply when a person remains visibly in the loop and the process is transparent, and falls when the system operates as a black box, automated or not.</p><p>The scale of what is riding on getting this right is not small. The same Dice survey found 30% of tech professionals are considering leaving the industry entirely over hiring-process frustration — 14% actively exploring other careers, 15% seriously considering it. A hiring process that erodes trust does not only cost the rejected candidate; on this evidence it is pushing qualified people out of the applicant pool altogether.</p><h2>The Honest Summary</h2><p>Hiring is the clearest case in this entire series for a simple rule: disclosure and a visible human decision-maker are not a compliance afterthought, they are the entire difference between an AI hiring tool that is trusted and one that is not. The technology's accuracy or sophistication barely registers in the numbers above — a 70/8 trust gap is not a data-quality problem. It is what happens when one side of a decision can see how it was made and the other cannot. Every recommendation in this series' overview piece — disclose what's automated, keep a person genuinely in the loop, make the reasoning explainable — applies here with the least ambiguity of any sector we looked at.</p><p><a href="/blog/can-you-trust-ai-with-your-data.html">Back to the overview, and the other two pieces in this series</a>.</p><h3>Sources cited in this article</h3><ul><li>Greenhouse, <em>An AI Trust Crisis: AI in Hiring</em>, November 2025 (n=4,136: ~2,900 job seekers, ~1,236 recruiters/hiring managers; US, UK, Ireland, Germany)</li><li>Gartner, survey of 2,918 job candidates on AI screening and fairness, July 2025</li><li>ResumeBuilder, survey of 948 business leaders on AI hiring bias, October 2024</li><li>Dice, <em>The Trust Gap in Tech Hiring 2025</em> (n=319 US tech professionals, June–July 2025)</li></ul>
$ARTBODY$), 'deployed'
FROM pages p, content_components cc
WHERE p.site_id='4851f6fc-71cf-4160-a270-e03d6d3e0732' AND p.name='ai-data-trust-in-hiring-and-hr' AND cc.name='article-body';

INSERT INTO page_components (id, page_id, slot_name, position, component_id, content_data, build_status)
SELECT gen_random_uuid(), p.id, 'call-to-action', 3, cc.id, $CTAJSON${"headline": "Building or evaluating an AI system that decides things about people?", "subheadline": "Disclosure and a visible human decision-maker are the difference between a trusted system and a distrusted one -- we design that in from the start. Contact us at leopardess@contactforsales.com or call +44 (0) 7934 524 911.", "primary_cta": "Start with an architecture conversation", "secondary_cta": "See how the platform runs in production"}$CTAJSON$::jsonb, 'deployed'
FROM pages p, content_components cc
WHERE p.site_id='4851f6fc-71cf-4160-a270-e03d6d3e0732' AND p.name='ai-data-trust-in-hiring-and-hr' AND cc.name='call-to-action';

SELECT p.name, count(pc.id) FROM pages p JOIN page_components pc ON pc.page_id=p.id
WHERE p.site_id='4851f6fc-71cf-4160-a270-e03d6d3e0732' AND p.name='ai-data-trust-in-hiring-and-hr' GROUP BY p.name;
COMMIT;
